n children as a kind of  currency and make liberal use  of evil curses before the brothers grim  there was an italian writer giambattista  basili who spent the late 15th  and early 16th century creating and  collecting what are now known as some of  the oldest recorded fairy tales  one of his stories is called sun moon  and talia  and it later went on to serve as  inspiration for the tale we now know as  sleeping beauty  in this version a lord is told that his  daughter will be endangered by a piece  of flocks  so he bans the plant from his home his  daughter talia spins flocks with an old  woman  and winds up with a piece of it under  her fingernail which is enough to send  her into a seemingly unwakeable slumber  don't you just hate it when that happens  in sun moon antalya  the old woman isn't purposefully evil or  petty nor does she turn into a dragon at  any point  so she's clearly quite different from  most versions of maleficent we know but  it's not hard to see the inspiration  here  next up we have charles perrault another  famous author and fairy tale enthusiast  who has his own version of the tale  called the sleeping beauty in the wood  unlike gian batista basili's version  this one involves a number of good  fairies  eager to heap good fortune on a princess  and one old fairy who feels left out at  the party  the older fairy doesn't take too kindly  to being overlooked and decides to curse  the princess  to prick her finger on a spinning wheel  and pass away as a result  the remaining fairy negates the curse  enough so the princess falls asleep  instead  and the disgruntled fairy isn't seen  again and presumably  is not invited to any more parties now  let's take a look at little briar rose  from  jacob ludwig carl grimm and wilhelm carl  grimm  better known as the brothers grim in  this version  the king throws a celebration for the  birth of his daughter but with a limit  on the amount of fairies he can invite  due to the fact that he only had 12  golden plates a hosting dilemma that  we're all familiar with  so when the 13th fairy inevitably showed  up  she was livid about the lack of invite  and hurled a curse at the princess which  was then negated as much as possible by  another story  this version of the wicked fairy is very  similar to the one in the sleeping  beauty in the wood  and should inspire us all to consult our  guest lists a little more carefully  [Music]  it's not hard to see the similarities  between these fairy tales and how they  relate to more modern versions of the  story but there's an even earlier  version that exists  yet it's so often overlooked because  it's a romantic story  rather than a traditional fairy tale  purse of forest was written in french  anonymously  sometime between 1330 and 1345 and  wasn't translated until much later  instead of fairies the king invited  three goddesses to a party but  forgot to give themis the god of destiny  a knife what is it with these royals not  being able to pull off a proper party  themis may have been the goddess of  destiny but she clearly appreciates good  hospitality because she cursed the  queen's child to fall asleep after  pricking her finger  and not awaken until the splinter was  removed themis  may actually be the earliest version of  the character we know as maleficent  and she isn't just from this one  particular tale in greek mythology  themis was a titan who was often  associated with divine law  justice and customs and unlike  maleficent she wasn't known for being  mean or petty  quite the contrary themis wasn't  wrathful at all  and if she was disregarded then it was  the goddess nemesis who would step into  dolo punishments it's possible that more  modern versions of maleficent are  actually the result of a combination of  themis  and nemesis and that sleeping beauty  really dates back to the epic greek poem  dionysica  if you thought your vhs copy of disney's  sleeping beauty was old keep in mind  that this hails  from the late 4th or 5th century will  share a very um abridged version of this  20  426 line poem yeah the minor deity aura  got on the wrong side of artemis the  goddess of the hunt  so artemis asked nemesis to help her get  revenge  and nemesis convinced eros god of love  to cause dionysus to fall for aura  ariadne gives dionysus a disstaff to  present  aura with a disstaff being an early  version of what we now know today as a  spinning wheel  it's hard not to see the similarities  between nemesis and maleficent or the  fact that aura and aurora  sound quite similar and their stories  both involve a perceived slight  revenge and a spinning wheel type device  so in reality  the concept of a character like  maleficent is way older than most people  realize  thinking you could defeat me me the  mistress of all  evil disney's animated movie sleeping  beauty was released in 1959  and though it's considered a classic now  it actually did poorly at the box office  and represented disney's first annual  loss in over a decade at that point but  that's because of the high production  cost and mixed reviews from critics  not maleficent herself at least we think  in this version of the story  maleficent is much more of a villain  than in earlier versions of the story  in sun moon and talia she was just an  old woman that happened to facilitate a  spinning wheel mishap  then in the sleeping beauty in the wood  and little briar rose  she showed up at a party felt scorned  and set forth a curse that was in some  way mitigated by a less  salty fairy but in this disney version  maleficent is  much more insidious the maleficent most  of us know doesn't settle for cursing  princess aurora after she's not invited  to her christening she then seeks out  aurora  lures her into a room and transforms  into a spinning wheel for her to prick  her finger on  that's a lot more than previous versions  but  not quite enough to earn the nickname  mistress of evil  so maleficent captures prince philip and  imprisons him  then later becomes a dragon summons a  forest of thorns  and only stops after being stuck by  philip's sword of truth  disney's animated version of the  character was the cruelest up until that  point in time the princess  shall indeed grow in grace and beauty  yet disney was also responsible for  somewhat redeeming maleficent with their  live-action version  in previous versions of the story we've  seen the curse happy  seek revenge after being slighted in  ways that seem a little dramatic if  we're being honest  but the live-action maleficent actually  gave us a lot of backstory about the  character and why she had such a serious  grudge against the king  in this version she fell in love with a  peasant named stefan but eventually the  two grew apart  later stefan betrayed maleficent by  cutting off her wings to win the favor  of king henry as well as the crown for  himself  all of a sudden her missing party  invitation makes a lot more sense  and so does the fact that she shows up  anyway and curses king stefan's daughter  aurora and unlike previous versions of  the story  maleficent's spell isn't mitigated by  another fairy she makes it so true  love's kiss will break it which would  have been even nicer if she believed  love existed at the time in the end  maleficent comes to love aurora as a  daughter and it's her feelings that  ultimately free the princess from the  curse  even though this may be the most  powerful we've seen maleficent it's also  the most human we've seen this mighty  fairy her motivations are understandable  and she comes across as less petty and  obsessed with proper dinner party  etiquette than she does in other  versions  but don't worry i'll see you at the  christening  in the descendants we see a mighty  maleficent but she's as  evil as ever and just wants to use her  daughter mal as part of her wicked plans  we definitely don't want to see a sweet  mother-daughter type relationship like  we did between maleficent and 